< The Antikythera mechanism is a calculator of the heavens made of bronze gears. It was recovered in 1901 from a shipwreck of the first century BC, off the Greek island of Antikythera, and only about a third of it survives, in 82 fragments. As scholars have reconstructed it, pointers on its front showed where the Sun, the Moon and five planets stood in the sky, and a dial on its back showed which months might bring an eclipse. I took the reconstructions as published and asked what the machine would show today if it had been kept turning for two thousand two hundred years. In short: its pointers for Mercury, Mars and Jupiter would be nearly as good as new (which was never very good), and its Moon pointer would be nearly a third of a turn out.
