> The cause is a slip. Every eclipse happens near one of the two points where the Moon's path crosses the Sun's, and each cycle slips about half a degree against those points. So the marked months lose their eclipses one after another, and a month that has lost its eclipse never gets one back. Meanwhile eclipses of the Moon that the eye can see, partial or total ones, turn up in months without a mark, and in time the dial missed every one of them.
